# Line endings are normalised in the repository, and that has to be declared HERE.
#
# `git status` is what the container build uses to decide whether an image can be
# tied to a commit (see `awltux.buildinfo` and the builder stage of the Dockerfile).
# On Windows, Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` in its *system* config, so
# CRLF files match LF blobs and the tree looks clean. The container has no such
# config, so the same files were compared raw and six of them reported modified --
# which branded **every** image a dirty build no matter how carefully the work was
# committed. The Info page then printed the correct commit next to "has uncommitted
# changes", which is worse than printing nothing: it says the deploy cannot be
# trusted when it can, so the warning stops meaning anything.
#
# `text=auto` makes the normalisation a property of the repository rather than of
# the machine that happens to be checking it out. Git decides per file -- anything
# with a NUL byte near the start is left alone as binary -- and normalises line
# endings on the way in and out, so a CRLF working tree and an LF blob compare equal
# wherever the check runs.
* text=auto
